around five point five thousand years  ago the bowtie people in northern  Kazakhstan began riding and milking  horses this was the beginning of horse  domestication which allowed humans to  travel much faster connect to vast  territories and develop advanced warfare  since then horse domestication has  transformed civilization but how exactly  have the transformed the horse recently  researchers sequenced the genomes of  Inchon horses from four point one to two  point three thousand years old  thirteen samples come from the royal  burial sites of Skippy's nomads who  ruled much of Central Asia  during the Iron Age the barrel site is a  rare snapshot of a horse population  eleven samples all from the same  generation sacrifice the same day as a  part of a funeral ritual and almost  perfectly preserved in permafrost these  samples tell us a lot about Skippy ins  and their horses first the genomes show  that the horses in their herds had a  variety of prose colors Skippy endorses  carry diverse mutation is relating to  speed states and endurance some of them  had gene variants found in today's  fastest racing horses while others had  gene variants associated with stamina  researchers found 121 genes selected by  schedules including a large number  involved in the development of the legs  of horses it seems Skippy and breeders  selected for strong lay  examination of the bones from the burial  site and other skin Ian's remains  confirm this finding by comparing  ancient genomes with present-day genomes  researchers came away with new insights  on horse domestication and animal  domestication in general Skippy and  horses had a variety of y chromosomes  which means male lineages in the first  3,000 years of domestication were  diverse modern samples however show that  breeders seem to use less and less of a  variety of males up to the point that  today's horses all carry virtually the  same Y chromosome this tells us that the  horse population has collapsed within  the last twenty three hundred years  which resulted in a reduction of horse  genetic diversity at the whole genome  scale during the same time period  breeding practices must have led to the  introduction of harmful mutations  because modern horses show an abundance  of these mutations while 15 horses don't  looking at the changes in horse genomes  also gives support for a domestication  hypothesis involving neural crest cells  because these cells develop into many  different tissues and cell lineages  changes in them might explain why many  domestic animals share a group of traits  like floppy ears and various coat  problems  so studying the genomes of skip Ian's  horses is starting to bring these  ancient animals to life but it'll take  many many more samples and from all  along the timeline and across the globe  to tell the whole story of horse  domestication  [Music]  you  [Music]
 

